  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n830AM update: Swath of heavy rain is following along and just to\nthe north of the track of an MCV that is now approaching the\nnorthern WV panhandle. KPBZ 88D has been underestimating\nrainfall totals at times, with some 2 to 3 inch totals in 2 to 3\nhours occurring along the heavier band. A few Flash Flood\nWarnings have already been issued, and given the expected ENE\ntrack of the MCV, have expanded the Flood Watch to the east to\nencompass several more counties. Still believe that the threat\nlargely ends by early afternoon as the MCV exits.\n\nPrevious discussion...\n\nDriven by a 700mb shortwave/moisture plume over a stalled\nsurface boundary, showers with limited thunderstorms are \nunderway from eastern OH to south central IN and will advance\neast northeast through the region through the early afternoon\ntoday. The combination of moisture influx pushing PWATs toward\nthe 90th-95th percentile (based on latest HREF data/RAP\nmesoanalysis and a noted uptick from prior HREF mean values)\nplus jet-aided ascent lends to a narrow band of heavier rain\n(2-4\" total range) that may result in localized flash flooding,\nevidenced by the Flood Watch. Timing remains on track: eastern\nOH sees heavy rain expansion between 3am-5am, intensities\npeaking between 5am-10am, and rain ending around noon; northern\nWV and western PA is roughly +2 hours to that timeline.\n\nThough very small pockets of heavier rain could occur outside\nthe watch area, slightly higher FFG thresholds due to less\nsaturated grounds is enough to not offer NE expansion of watch\neven as guidance suggest these totals may also extend into west\ncentral PA. The focus, then, remains on the I-70 corridor (and\njust slightly south) as the most probable axis from 2\"+ totals\nwithin 10kms in the 15-30% range.  \n\nConfidence remains high that the flash flood threat ends fairly\nquickly as the shortwave exits by the early afternoon, through\nresidual lift/moisture may still cause a stray shower into the\nevening with little hazardous risk.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nSubtle trends in a weak surface high development Friday suggests\nthat the previous expectation of areawide dry weather may be too\nbold and that the combination of diurnal heating, trough-induced\nlift aloft, and enough area moisture could result in isolated to\nscattered storms during the afternoon/evening mainly east of\nI-77.\n\nThat then serves as a precursor to additional shortwave trough\nmovement aloft combining with a more defined surface cold front\nthat passes through sometime Saturday afternoon into Sunday.\nVariances in upper trough depth/positioning and timing of the\nwave makes it difficult to assess potential storm environments\nand potential hazardous weather threats. Ensemble guidance,\ndriven primarily by Canadian modeling, does show scenarios with\nenough CAPE/dCAPE for a damaging wind threat (though weak shear\nwon't be a boon) that seems to be captured by a few other \nmachine-learning convective outlook models. This scenario is \nmainly tied to clean heating prior to the front/shortwave and \nSaturday afternoon/early evening storm occurrence; earlier/later\narrivals or more cloud cover is just as likely that would \ndampen or completely squash that risk. Trends will be monitored \nto understand how things may track.\n\nConfidence remains fairly high that high pressure underneath\nweakening upper troughing favors drier weather for the first\nhalf of next week.",
